Code a program makes at run time gets code caches by its bytes (FEX 21ad9d8, on top of 0035)

NFSU2 with sound on runs its race at 10-20 FPS on an iPad (44 without).
EA's sound system (SNDSYS v9.03.04, linked into SPEED2.EXE) loads x86
modules (mixers, decoders, effects) into the heap: its loader at SPEED2
0x7212e5 lays out each module, VirtualProtects every section to
PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE (5 modules in the menu, about 57 sections in a
race) and fixes up addresses in it. That code is in no image, so the AOT
image had nothing for it and FEX's interpreter ran all of it. The layout
is the same in every run, at the same addresses.

A range a VirtualProtect makes executable outside the images is now
remembered with its exact bounds (the notification before the call has
them; the one after has them rounded to pages, and the rest of a heap
page is other data). When its first block misses, the frontend asks the
WoW64 module to prepare it (QueryRuntimeCode, PrepareRuntimeCode; called
without the invalidation lock). ImageTracker then hashes its bytes with
its guest address into an id and registers the range as an image named
rtcode-<guest address>-<id>.fexrc. To FEX it is then an image like any
other:
- the code map records its blocks under that id, and the range's bytes
  go to codemap\new\runtime\<name> next to the new code maps;
- the offline compiler's process-all moves those files to runtime\ in
  its cache directory, and compiling the name loads the bytes back at
  the guest address, executable and writable as they were;
- the AOT image holds the resulting cache under the id, and a later run
  loads it when the same bytes are at the same address.
Other bytes at a known range (another module) give another id. A range
made non-executable or freed is forgotten.

Writes to other data on a page of such code drop its blocks (the page is
write-protected while it holds code). A miss in a range whose cache was
loaded, at a block the cache had, hashes the range again; with the same
bytes the cache's blocks are registered again ("[rt-code] ... registered
again after an invalidation"), otherwise the range gets a new id.

The ready map of an executable lists the libraries process-all compiles
with it. A runtime range is recorded as a library of the executable that
loaded it, and each run's code map lists the ranges of that run (other
addresses or bytes give other ids), so the ready map now keeps the
libraries of every code map imported for it and of its previous version,
and is rewritten when a code map brings a new library even without new
blocks. Before, only the last code map's libraries were kept and ranges
of other runs were never compiled.

